  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nSome very weak elevated instability and mid level moisture will move \nthrough the area this afternoon and early evening. Very light \nshowers and sprinkles remain possible during this time with no \nimpacts expected.\n\nWinds decrease through the night with temperatures expected to fall \nback into the low to mid 40s, with a few spots in the upper 30s. The \nbetter chance for lows in the 30s will be near highway 14 and in the \nmid James Valley. For now will not issue a frost advisory as some of \nthe models are hinting that a stratus deck around 1500 feet could \ndevelop later tonight which would limit the radiational cooling and \nfrost potential.  \n\nBroad warm advection increases on Wednesday night ahead of a wave \nmoving into NE. This forcing continues through a good portion of \nThursday, so expect rain late Wednesday night into Thursday. \nInstability pretty negligible so do not expected any thunderstorms. \nRainfall amounts should be highest south of I-90 where a quarter to \na half an inch should be more common. The latest Canadian Ens, EC \nEns and GEFS show about a 30 to 60 percent chance to exceed a half \nan inch south of I-90.\n\nThursday will become breezy with cool temperatures as rain comes to \nan end in the afternoon and evening. Highs should mostly be in the \n50s.\n\nWhile Saturday morning will be on the cold side, there are still \ncome concerns that southerly return flow could set up early enough \nFriday night to avoid widespread frost and freeze. Right now, \nnorthwest IA and nearby locations appear to see the best chance for \nlight winds late Friday night into Saturday morning and thus the \nbetter chance for frost. \n\nWeak ridging and westerly flow aloft will develop Saturday into \nSunday, bringing temperatures back to near to above normal during \nthis time. Upper level ridging strengthens early next week and \ncontinues through mid week as a large trough deepens over the \nRockies. This should support above normal temperatures and mainly \ndry conditions.",
